9.Identify the growth rates of the following code fragments using the Big-Oh notation: (6 marks) 
	sum   0;
for (int i = n; i > 0; i /= 2)
for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)
sum  sum +1;
	sum  0;
for (int i = 1; i < n; i *= 2)
for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)
sum  sum +1;

a. sum ← 0;
for (int i = n; i > 0; i /= 2)
    for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)
        sum ← sum + 1;
Answer
There are two loops:
The outer loop changes i 
The inner loop runs i times 
Step 1: Analyze the outer loop
The statement: i /= 2
means i is divided by 2 every iteration.
So, the values of i become: n, n/2, n/4, n/8……….
The loop stops when i becomes 0.
Since we keep having the value, the outer loop runs about: log2 n times.
Step 2: Analyze the inner loop
The inner loop is:
for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)
This runs exactly i times for each outer iteration. 
Step 3: Add total work
Total operations: n+ n/2+ n/4+ n/8+……….
This is a geometric series.
For geometric series: n+ n/2+ n/4+ n/8+………. =2n
So, the total work is proportional to:2n
In Big-Oh notation, constants are ignored
So, the growth rate is: O(n)
b.sum ← 0;
for (int i = 1; i < n; i *= 2)
    for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)
        sum ← sum + 1;
Answer
Step 1: Analyze the outer loop
The outer loop is:
for (int i = 1; i < n; i *= 2)
here i is multiplied by 2 every iteration.
Values of i become:1,2,4,8,16………
These are powers of 2:20,21,22,23
Suppose the loop runs k times. Then after k iterations:
i=2k
The loop stops when i ≥ n.
So:2k>=n

Taking log base 2 on both sides: 2k>=n
                             k>=log2n
	               

		 
Therefore, the number of iterations is approximately: log2n
The statement: i *= 2
means i doubles every iteration.
Values of i become:1,2,4,8,16……………….
The loop stops when i ≥ n.
So, the outer loop runs about: log2n times.
Step 2: Analyze the inner loop
The inner loop runs i times.
Step 3: Add total work
Total operations: 1+2+4+8+……………+n
This is another geometric series: 1+2+4+8+……………+n=2n-1
So, the total work is proportional to:2n
In Big-Oh notation, constants are ignored
So, the growth rate is: O(n)
10. Determine the growth rates for the following expressions using the Big-Oh notation: (6 marks) 
	log (n2 + 1) / log(n) 
	log (n400 + n2 + n + 90) 
	n/2 + c
1. log (n2 + 1) / log(n) 
Answer 
In log (n2 + 1) / log(n) dominant term
log (n2 + 1) = log(n2) 
Using log rules: log(n2) =2logn
2logn/log(n)=2
A constant has growth rate :O (1)

2. log (n400 + n2 + n + 90) 
Answer
The dominant term inside the logarithm is:  n400
using log rules
 log(n400) = 400log(n)                                     
Constant factors are ignored in Big-Oh notation
So, the growth rate is: O (log n)
3. n /2+c
Answer
In n /2+c the dominant term is: n
Constant factors are ignored in Big-Oh notation
So, the growth rate is: O(n)
